WEBVTT

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:05.744
<v Rohan>Where are we now? Everything looks so old!

00:00:05.744 --> 00:00:13.069
<v Mishu>In seventeen ninety-one, France began the metric system, with the metre, the kilogram and the second.

00:00:13.069 --> 00:00:17.629
<v Ishita>But how do you keep a metre safe? You can't put a length in a box.

00:00:17.629 --> 00:00:25.809
<v Mishu>The metre was kept as two fine marks on a special metal bar in Paris, and exact copies were sent all over the world.

00:00:25.709 --> 00:00:30.286
<v Mishu>Today, scientists define the metre using the speed of light.

00:00:30.286 --> 00:00:32.583
<v Rohan>Did one of those copies come to India?

00:00:32.583 --> 00:00:36.965
<v Mishu>India's copy is kept at the National Physical Laboratory in New Delhi.

00:00:36.865 --> 00:00:41.727
<v Mishu>The metre rods and kilograms in our markets are copies of these standards.

00:00:41.727 --> 00:00:44.478
<v Ishita>Did our markets always use kilograms?

00:00:44.478 --> 00:00:48.170
<v Mishu>Some more extra info. It may not be in your textbook.

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:52.571
<v Mishu>Before, the measures in India's markets could change from town to town.

00:00:52.471 --> 00:01:03.311
<v Mishu>In nineteen fifty-six, India passed a law to use the metric system, and from nineteen fifty-eight, our markets began to change over to metres and kilograms.

00:01:03.311 --> 00:01:14.404
<v Mishu>And now everyone writes units the same way too: four c m, with small letters, a space after the number, no full stop, and never c m s.

00:01:14.404 --> 00:01:17.531
<v Rohan>Oops. I always wrote c m s!

00:01:17.531 --> 00:01:19.082
<v Mishu>Not any more!

00:01:19.082 --> 00:01:24.338
<v Mishu>A tailor in Delhi, a tailor in Chennai: the same cloth, the same answer.

00:01:24.238 --> 00:01:29.300
<v Mishu>That's the end of our tour. Now you know why the whole world agrees!

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:31.577
<v Ishita>Same cloth, same answer!
